cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Attention-decay in pandemic surveillance is an emerging-disease phenomenon"
message: "If you reference this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Tufte
    given-names: Taggart
    affiliation: "Department of Mathematical Sciences, Montana State University"
    email: taggarttufte@gmail.com
date-released: 2026-04-26
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A multi-signal evaluation of wastewater, search-query, information-seeking,
  and outpatient-syndromic surveillance signals across COVID-19 (2021-2024)
  and influenza (2021-2025). Finds that attention-based surveillance signals
  (Google Trends, Wikipedia) show 5-23x variance compression after the first
  major COVID-19 wave but show no compression across flu seasons, localizing
  the failure mode to the emerging-disease novelty cycle. Wastewater is the
  only signal type with stable variance across both diseases. Includes a
  negative result on LLM-prompt surveillance with WildChat-4.8M.
keywords:
  - pandemic surveillance
  - wastewater epidemiology
  - anomaly detection
  - early warning
  - multi-signal fusion
  - attention decay
  - COVID-19
  - influenza
